+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | Draft                |
+
+Tool definitions already expose JSON Schema to providers; the same schema is the authority for deciding whether model-supplied input may reach a tool executor.
+
+## Registry contract
+
+- Before invoking an executor, `ToolRegistry.execute` validates input against the resolved tool definition's `inputSchema`; rejected input returns a `ToolResponse` that identifies the failing property path and constraint without including property values in either the response or logs, while valid input (including unknown properties) reaches the executor once and unchanged, and unknown tool names continue to throw ([validated by: rejects malformed input without executing or disclosing values, but passes valid input through unchanged](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L32)).
+- Registration compiles a static definition's schema immediately and reuses that compiled schema across executions, naming the tool when compilation fails; a function-valued definition is resolved for every call and validated against that call's schema, caching compilation only by resolved schema-object identity ([validated by: compiles static schemas at registration and resolves dynamic schemas for each call](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L76)).
+
+## Conversation contract
+
+- Schema rejection is an ordinary tool result rather than an exception: the orchestrator gives it to the provider on the next bounded tool round, allowing the provider to correct the input and complete the message ([validated by: returns a rejection to the provider so it can correct the call within the tool loop](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L111)).
+
+## Out of scope
+
+Tool output validation, authorization, executor retry and timeout policies, and changing the unknown-tool error are separate concerns.
